BABFT's servos allow for an input angle of rotation of up to 90 degrees.
Servos can additionally hold these positions when activated with a switch. 
However, servos will not function if they are attatched to a mass which is too heavy, and can only be activated by a single set of inputs from a driver seat.
This becomes more apparent when two servos are directly connected together (which would be done for rotation of masses >90 degrees, or if the mass needs to rotate in two seperate directions from the same driver seat).
Servos act this way as they cannot exert a strong enough torque to rotate larger masses.
This video explores three designs for alternate contraptions for rotation in BABFT:

Rapid reflex joint:
The front wheel can rotate left/right 30 degrees in addition to the wheel spinning.
The hinges connecting the wheel to it's block prevent the wheel from spinning while allowing it to turn left/right.

Wide angle joint (wheel):
Classic wheels exert a greater torque than servos in BABFT.
The torque of several wheels can be combined for rotation of any angle within a specified range (as allowed by the bar perpendicular to it, which would prevent rotations above an input amount)

Wide angle joint (servos):
The same effect as with wheels can be achieved with servos.
However, significantly more servos would be needed (therefore more expensive) and this joint is more susceptible to failing.

